RISC OS: sound through UnixLib's /dev/dsp.

The Linux version loads a sound plug-in with dlopen and plays it through
ALSA; RISC OS has neither, so the port was silent.
platform/riscos/fsairsoundriscos.cpp now does the plug-in's job itself:
- loads the same WAV files (sounddll/yswavfile, now built into the RISC OS
  platform library), converted once to 16-bit mono 22050 Hz;
- picks the looping sound as upstream's ALSA plug-in does (gun, else alarm,
  else the engine at one of 10 power levels) and mixes up to 4 one-shot
  sounds over it (the ALSA plug-in played only one thing at a time);
  a new engine level carries on at the same place in the loop;
- writes to /dev/dsp opened with O_NONBLOCK, from FsSoundKeepPlaying (once a
  frame), keeping 160 ms queued and never offering more than GETOSPACE
  says fits, so the game never waits for the sound;
- the device is opened only when there's something to play, and closed
  (queue dropped) by FsSoundStopAll and when sound is switched off. If it
  can't be opened, that's logged and tried again 200 frames later.
UnixLib (5.0.3.3) plays /dev/dsp through SharedSoundBuffer when its
modules are loaded, else DigitalRenderer. No voice: upstream's voice
plug-in is an empty skeleton.
Host test: tests/host-sound (fake /dev/dsp with a playback clock).

