+/* RISC OS port: YSFlight's sound through UnixLib's /dev/dsp.
+
+   The Linux version loads its sound plug-in with dlopen and plays through
+   ALSA; RISC OS has neither. This file does the plug-in's job in the
+   program itself: it loads the same WAV files, mixes the looping
+   "environment" sound (engine, gun or alarm) with the one-shot sounds,
+   and writes 16-bit mono at 22050 Hz to /dev/dsp. UnixLib plays that
+   through SharedSoundBuffer when its modules are loaded (mixed with other
+   programs' sound), else through DigitalRenderer.
+
+   FsSoundKeepPlaying() is called once a frame. Each call tops the queue
+   up to TARGET_MS of sound, never waiting: /dev/dsp is opened with
+   O_NONBLOCK, and no more is written than GETOSPACE says fits. One-shot
+   sounds are mixed into what is written next, so they start within the
+   queued time (at most TARGET_MS).
+
+   There's no voice output: upstream's voice plug-in is an empty skeleton. */
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
